## What I'd change and why

The code above satisfies the functional requirement exactly: each line with a null `Discount` gets the standard discount, and any line that already has a value (including `0`) is left alone. Rules stay in `PricingRules`—nothing is copied.

**However, at 200 lines this will hit the 100-SOQL governor limit.** `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(Id)` performs a `[SELECT Family FROM Product2 WHERE Id = :productId]` on every call. Calling it in a loop for 200 null-discount lines issues 200 SOQL queries in a single transaction, which throws a `QueryException` well past the limit of 100.

**Recommended fix (no changes to `PricingRules`, no rule duplication):**

In the handler, batch the lookups while still delegating all discount logic to `PricingRules`:

```apex
public static void handleBeforeInsert(List<OpportunityLineItem> newLines) {
    // 1. Collect the Product2Id values that actually need a discount.
    Set<Id> neededProductIds = new Set<Id>();
    for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLines) {
        if (line.Discount == null) {
            neededProductIds.add(line.Product2Id);
        }
    }
    if (neededProductIds.isEmpty()) { return; }

    // 2. ONE SOQL to get the families (1 query regardless of line count).
    Map<Id, Product2> products = new Map<Id, Product2>(
        [SELECT Id, Family FROM Product2 WHERE Id IN :neededProductIds]
    );

    // 3. Call PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily for each distinct product.
    //    This keeps every discount rule in PricingRules—nothing is copied.
    Map<Id, Decimal> discountByProduct = new Map<Id, Decimal>();
    for (Product2 p : products.values()) {
        discountByProduct.put(p.Id, PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(p.Family));
    }

    // 4. Assign.
    for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLines) {
        if (line.Discount == null) {
            line.Discount = discountByProduct.get(line.Product2Id);
        }
    }
}
```

**Why this is better:**

| Concern | Loop version | Batched version |
|---|---|---|
| SOQL queries (200 lines) | **200** → governor blow-up | **1** |
| DML / governor headroom | none left for downstream triggers | ample |
| Rule ownership | `PricingRules` | still `PricingRules` (`standardDiscountForFamily`) |
| Code duplication | none | none |
| CPQ 200-line quotes | ✗ fails | ✓ works |

I used `standardDiscountForFamily` rather than `standardDiscountFor` specifically because the latter hides a per-call SOQL. Calling `standardDiscountForFamily` is not "copying rules"—it is the other public method on the class the pricing team exposed, and every discount decision still flows through `PricingRules`. If the pricing team ever changes the family → discount mapping, this handler picks it up automatically.

If the pricing team ever adds a genuine batch API (e.g. `standardDiscountFor(Set<Id>)`) you would switch to that and drop the intermediate map, but given the current `PricingRules` surface, the pattern above is the smallest, safest change.

